(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-diagnosing-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Diagnosing Infections — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Into which three categories do methods of identifying unknown microbes fall?","choices":["Bacterial, viral and fungal","Phenotypic, genotypic and immunological","Microscopic, macroscopic and chemical","Direct, indirect and confirmatory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and almost every technique can be placed by asking which of the three it belongs to — observable characteristics, genetic makeup, or antibody-antigen reactions. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does a precipitation test require?","choices":["A thermal cycler","Sensitized sheep red blood cells","A gel or liquid matrix","A plastic card or glass slide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — soluble antigen is made insoluble by antibody, and a visible precipitate forms. Tube precipitation and Ouchterlony double-diffusion are the named forms. The precipitate forms within it. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Who invented the original electrophoretic blotting technique, and when?","choices":["Robert Koch, around 1890","Edwin Southern, around 1975","Kary Mullis, around 1983","Kirby and Bauer, around 1966"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, to detect specific DNA sequences in DNA samples — the Northern, Western and Eastern blots all take their names from his. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the Northern blot used to study?","choices":["Post-translational products such as lipids","Gene expression, by detecting RNA","Specific DNA sequences","Specific proteins in tissue extracts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is useful in the study of oncogenes and in rejection of transplanted organs. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Into which two categories do results fall?","choices":["Positive or negative","Direct or indirect","Sensitive or specific","Presumptive or confirmatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters clinically because a presumptive result may be enough to start treatment while confirmation follows. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What do physiological and biochemical characteristics detect?","choices":["The chemical composition of the cell wall","Colony pigment and texture","The sequence of the 16S ribosomal RNA gene","The presence or absence of enzymes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, read as physiological reactions to nutrients. Catalase, oxidase, coagulase and the hemolysins are among the common tests. Metabolic pathways are read the same way. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is used in fluorescent antibody testing?","choices":["A radioactive isotope label","An enzyme producing a colored chromogen","Sensitized sheep red blood cells","A dye-labeled monoclonal antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, used to visualize cells or cell aggregates by either direct or indirect methods. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What must be considered about any organism recovered from a specimen?","choices":["Whether it is Gram positive or negative","Whether the specimen was collected aseptically","Whether it is causing disease or is normal flora","Whether it grew faster than expected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is where Koch's postulates come back in — recovering an organism is not the same as showing it is responsible. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does phenotype mean in this context?","choices":["The antibody response the organism provokes","The organism's position on a phylogenetic tree","The physical expression of genes","The complete genetic makeup of the organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why phenotypic methods rest on what can be observed — cell shape, colony appearance, enzyme activity — rather than on the genes themselves. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does MIC stand for?","choices":["Maximum inhibitory concentration","Mean infectious count","Microbial identification code","Minimum inhibitory concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and MIC strips report it directly as a number rather than as the zone diameter the disk-diffusion method produces. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is a zone of inhibition interpreted?","choices":["A larger zone indicates a more effective agent","A larger zone indicates a less effective agent","Zone size reflects how fast the organism grows","Zone size indicates the specimen was contaminated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and minimum inhibitory concentration strips give the same information as a concentration — the lowest level that stops growth. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is phage typing?","choices":["A phenotypic method using bacteriophage","A genotypic method sequencing viral DNA","An immunological method using labeled antibody","A susceptibility test measuring zones of inhibition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside animal inoculation — used for organisms such as Mycobacterium leprae — among the less common phenotypic approaches. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Why does faster diagnosis matter to the patient?","choices":["It guarantees the organism is the cause of disease","Proper treatment can begin in a timely manner","It reduces the cost of the laboratory test","It removes the need for sensitivity testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and precise automated methods deliver quick results — which is the clinical argument for genotypic testing over waiting for growth. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of these is a named common laboratory test?","choices":["Coagulase","Polymerase chain reaction","Western blot","Ouchterlony double-diffusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside catalase, oxidase, the hemolysins, carbohydrate fermentation, amino acid utilization, and hydrolysis of gelatin, starch or lipids. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What distinguishes in vivo testing from serology?","choices":["It requires the organism to be cultured first","It measures antibody titre in binding antibody units","Antigen is introduced directly into the body","It uses serum drawn from the patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, to determine the presence or absence of antibodies. The tuberculin skin test and allergy testing are the named examples. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What labels the antigen or antibody in a radioimmunoassay?","choices":["An enzyme producing a chromogen","A restriction enzyme","Radioactive isotopes","A fluorescent dye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the alternative is the enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, where an enzyme-antibody complex produces a colored chromogen instead. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why are serological methods described as very sensitive?","choices":["Serum contains large amounts of every antibody","The tests amplify the signal enzymatically in every case","They are always run in duplicate","Antibodies have extreme specificity for antigens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that specificity is what allows trace quantities to be detected — the basis of the immunoassays in particular. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why do viruses present special difficulties for diagnosis?","choices":["They cannot be detected by any immunological method","They grow too quickly to be captured","They have no nucleic acid to sequence","They are not cells, and need a host cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which makes them labor intensive to culture — and is why rapid point of care tests based on antigen-antibody reactions matter so much here. The host cell must be a specific type. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the major practical advantage of genotypic methods?","choices":["They cost less than phenotypic methods","They identify antibody titre as well","They require no specimen from the patient","Culture is not necessary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it matters most for slow-growing organisms such as Mycobacterium and difficult ones such as Legionella — the delay of culture is avoided entirely. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What limits the usefulness of a rapid immunological test?","choices":["You need a hypothesis to target it","It cannot detect antigen, only antibody","It always requires culture first","It takes longer than culture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fever, sore throat and pus-pockets on the tonsils lead you to suspect strep throat, and only then does the rapid test answer the question. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which stains are named for immediate direct examination?","choices":["Southern and Northern blots","Gram stain and acid-fast stain","Catalase and oxidase","Bromthymol blue and phenol red"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, together with direct fluorescent antibody and direct antigen testing — methods that report before anything has been cultured. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What visible reactions can serological tests produce?","choices":["Colony pigment on an agar plate","Bands on a phylogenetic tree","Precipitates, color change, radioactivity","Zones of inhibition around a disk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the tests report not only presence but AMOUNT — the titre, often given in binding antibody units. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which of these is an immunological method?","choices":["Acid-fast staining","Complement fixation","Restriction fragment length polymorphism","Carbohydrate fermentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside agglutination, precipitation, immunoelectrophoresis, immunofluorescence, immunoassays and in vivo allergy testing. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does RFLP stand for?","choices":["Restriction fragment length polymorphism","Ribosomal fragment linkage pattern","Rapid fluorescent labeled probe","Recombinant fragment ligation product"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is produced by hybridizing DNA probes complementary to sequences specific to a particular microbe — also called DNA fingerprinting. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What can be observed on a triple sugar iron slant?","choices":["Several pH changes","A single pH change only","Zones of inhibition around antibiotic disks","Precipitin lines in agar gel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes one tube informative about more than a single reaction — unlike urea broth, which reports one enzyme. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does mannitol salts agar do?","choices":["Selects on salt, differentiates mannitol","Detects hemolytic activity","Supports anaerobic culture","Detects citrate utilization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is both selective and differential at once, using a pH change to report the fermentation. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What instrument does the polymerase chain reaction use?","choices":["A thermal cycler","An electrophoresis tank","A 96-well plate reader","A fluorescence microscope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, to amplify specific bits of DNA or RNA. The basic concept was invented by Kary Mullis and colleagues. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does macroscopic morphology assess?","choices":["Antibody binding to whole cell antigen","Colony appearance on culture media","Cell shape and stain reaction","The organism's gene sequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — texture, size, shape, pigment and growth requirements, all observed with the naked eye rather than under the microscope. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does microscopic morphology examine?","choices":["The presence or absence of particular enzymes","Cell wall peptides and membrane lipids","Cell shape, size, stain reaction and cell structures","Colony texture, size, shape and pigment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, from fresh or stained microorganisms taken from the specimen. The Gram stain, flagellar stain and acid-fast stain are the named examples. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why are complement fixation results counter-intuitive?","choices":["The result is reported as a titre rather than positive or negative","Lysed red blood cells are a NEGATIVE result","Lysed red blood cells are a positive result","The test requires no antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If complement is fixed it cannot lyse the cells, so no hemolysis means the patient's serum is POSITIVE; hemolysis means it is negative. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
